{
  "title": "Tala pitha",
  "lead": "Follow the pulp into two distinct preparations.",
  "byline": "Ahimanikya Satapathy",
  "byline_role": "",
  "route": "/food/tala-pitha/",
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "baked",
      "text": "Odisha Food Delite heats water-extracted palm pulp, then stirs in rice powder, coconut, ghee and salt to form dough. It bakes, cools and slices the tala poda pitha. Fruit ripeness is unstated.",
      "source_ids": [
        "food-tala-delite2015"
      ],
      "note": 1,
      "kind": "sourced_summary"
    },
    {
      "id": "fried",
      "text": "Oriyarasoi starts with ripe fruit: squeezing and straining the flesh, then heating and cooling the extract. Rice powder and semolina make a dropping batter, fried in portions. This account describes tala pitha as fritters.",
      "source_ids": [
        "food-tala-oriyarasoi2016"
      ],
      "note": 2,
      "kind": "sourced_summary"
    }
  ],
  "notes": [
    {
      "number": 1,
      "source_id": "food-tala-delite2015",
      "text": "Odisha Food Delite, “Tala poda pitha,” 28 September 2015.",
      "link_text": "Tala poda pitha",
      "url": "https://odishafooddelite.wordpress.com/2015/09/28/talapalm-poda-pitha-%E0%AC%A4%E0%AC%BE%E0%AC%B3-%E0%AC%AA%E0%AD%8B%E0%AC%A1%E0%AC%BE-%E0%AC%AA%E0%AC%BF%E0%AC%A0%E0%AC%BE/"
    },
    {
      "number": 2,
